  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates probable outcomes based on the rule: an outcome is considered probable if its probability is higher than 1 divided by the number of lottery outcomes. Lottery A has three outcomes (2500 Euros with 33%, 2400 Euros with 66%, 0 Euros with 1%), so 1 divided by 3 is approximately 0.333. Thus, an outcome is probable if its probability > 0.333. In Lottery A: 2500 Euros (33% = 0.33) is not greater than 0.333, so not probable; 2400 Euros (66% = 0.66) > 0.333, so probable; 0 Euros (1% = 0.01) is not probable. Therefore, only the 2400 Euros outcome in A is probable. In Lottery B, there is only one outcome (2400 Euros with 100% probability), so 1 divided by 1 is 1.0. Since 100% = 1.0 is not greater than 1.0, the outcome is not considered probable under this rule. Thus, Lottery B has no probable outcomes. The reason prefers the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes. Since only Lottery A has one probable outcome (2400 Euros), and Lottery B has zero, A has a higher count of probable outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The decision reason evaluates probable outcomes using the threshold of 1/n, where n is the number of outcomes. For Lottery A (n=3), threshold is 1/3 ≈ 0.333. Only the 2400 Euros outcome (66%) exceeds this, so it is probable. For Lottery B (n=1), threshold is 1/1 = 1.0. The 2400 Euros outcome has 100% probability, which is not greater than 1.0, so it is not probable. Thus, Lottery A has one probable outcome, Lottery B has zero. The reason prefers the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes. Since A has more probable outcomes (1 vs 0), A is preferred.",
  "final_answer": "A"
}

